@meta
id: 23-04
title: Complete a whole line
chapter: 23
type: lesson
skills: complete-line
par: 27
minutes: 4
vscode: copy the line from above, paste it, fix the indent
open: order.repository.ts

@brief
`findRecentByCustomer` is half written. Two of its lines are stubs —
one starts `wh`, one starts `ret` — and each of them should be
identical to a line that already exists in `findByCustomer` above:

    where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));

Do not yank and paste. Completion can finish a line the same way it
finishes a word, and it fixes the indentation for you.

@keys
| `Ctrl-X Ctrl-L` (insert mode) | complete the whole line from the buffer |
| `Ctrl-L` again | next matching line |
| `Ctrl-N` / `Ctrl-P` | move through the candidates |

@hints
`/wh$` finds the first stub. `A` to append at its end, then the two
key chord.
--
`Ctrl-X` opens a sub-mode; `Ctrl-L` inside it means "lines". The
match is the nearest line above that starts with the same text.
--
`A` `Ctrl-X Ctrl-L` `Esc`, then `/ret$` and the same again.

@solution
    /wh$<Enter> A<Ctrl-X><Ctrl-L><Esc>
    /ret$<Enter> A<Ctrl-X><Ctrl-L><Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Twenty-three keystrokes for two lines that are 110 characters between
them.

`Ctrl-X` is a prefix. On its own it does nothing; it waits for a
second key that says what kind of completion you want. `Ctrl-L` is
lines: vim looks backward for a line that starts with what you have
typed so far, ignoring indentation, and inserts it — re-indented to
match the line you are on. That is why the stubs did not need to be
at the right column.

The nearest match above wins. If it is the wrong one, `Ctrl-L` again
moves to the next one further up, and `Ctrl-N` comes back. Use it for
the lines a codebase repeats constantly: `where` clauses, `@inject`
constructor lines, `expect(...)` calls in tests, import statements.

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public async findRecentByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      wh
      orderBy: { placedAt: 'desc' },
      take: 10,
    });

    ret
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      placedAt: record.placedAt,
    };
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public async findRecentByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
      orderBy: { placedAt: 'desc' },
      take: 10,
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      placedAt: record.placedAt,
    };
  }
}
